WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.300
A fellow knitter, Carmen, wants to knit
a cardigan with curved front edges.

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:12.980
Now, unlike diagonal shaping,
where you can use the magic formula,

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:20.460
there really isn't a mathematical formula
that can be used to create a deep curve.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.780
Here's a no math solution.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:26.460
Print out some knitter's graph paper,

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:31.340
preferably to-scale
graph paper, at your gauge.

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:33.920
Carmen's working at a gauge of 16 stitches

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:40.980
and 24 rows, so I created that size
paper from the Knit It Now tool.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:45.700
I've marked off 6 by 6 inches on the grid.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:50.320
Now, using to scale graph paper will
really help you visualize the size

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:56.420
of the curve and see if
that will work for your sweater.

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:59.960
Next, use a compass or a plate or just

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:05.720
wing it and draw a curve
between these two lines.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.040
I suggest making two of these and cut one

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:16.570
out and hold it up to see if the width
and the depth work for your design.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.930
Now, notice where the curve is
intersecting the grid lines.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:26.200
We'll establish our shaping
based on these points.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:33.160
Remember, each square represents
one stitch and one row.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.000
Use a pencil and start drawing a zigzag

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:46.100
line across the stitches and rows
following the curve.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:48.660
This doesn't have to be perfect.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.560
Can you see how this is creating a nice,

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:56.820
graceful curve following
along stitches and rows?

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:00.980
Here's something to consider when
you plan your actual knitting.

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:06.100
You'll be increasing stitches every row
and every other row,

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:11.680
so you want to consider where you're going
to place these increases depending on how

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:14.340
you're going to finish
the edge of your sweater.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:17.280
In other words,
do you want to work the increases

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:23.700
on the very edge stitches
or one or two stitches in from the edge?

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:27.440
Practice a bit to see what you
prefer before starting your garment.

